---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import BodyFatCalc from '../components/tools/BodyFatCalc';
const faq = [
  { q: "What measurements does the US Navy method need?", a: "Men need height, neck, and waist circumference. Women also add hip circumference. Measure the neck below the larynx, the waist at the navel for men (at the narrowest point for women), and the hips at the widest point — all in the same unit, kept snug but not compressing the skin." },
  { q: "How accurate is the Navy method?", a: "For most people it lands within about 3-4% of a DEXA or hydrostatic (underwater) reading. It relies on tape measurements rather than the actual density of your tissue, so results can drift for very lean, very muscular, or very heavy individuals. It is best used to track your own trend over time, not as a clinical diagnosis." },
  { q: "What is a healthy body fat percentage?", a: "The American Council on Exercise ranges are roughly: essential fat 2-5% (men) / 10-13% (women), athletes 6-13% / 14-20%, fitness 14-17% / 21-24%, average 18-24% / 25-31%, and obese 25%+ / 32%+. Some fat is essential for hormone production, temperature regulation, and organ protection." },
  { q: "Why do men and women use different formulas?", a: "Women naturally carry more essential fat (in the breasts, hips, and thighs), so the formula adds a hip measurement to capture that distribution. Applying the men's formula to a woman would systematically under-report her body fat." },
  { q: "Does body fat percentage change day to day?", a: "The underlying fat does not swing daily, but your tape measurements can, because water retention, a large meal, or bloating changes waist and hip girth. Measure at the same time of day (ideally in the morning, before eating) for the most consistent tracking." }
];
---
<ToolLayout title="Body Fat Percentage Calculator" description="Estimate body fat percentage using the US Navy method." slug="body-fat" faq={faq}>
  <BodyFatCalc client:load slot="tool" />
  <Fragment slot="tutorial">
    <h2>The US Navy Method Formula</h2>
    <p>This tool uses the US Navy circumference method, which estimates body fat from tape measurements and height rather than skinfold calipers. For men the formula is <code>%BF = 495 / (1.0324 − 0.19077 × log10(waist − neck) + 0.15456 × log10(height)) − 450</code>. For women it adds the hip measurement: <code>%BF = 495 / (1.29579 − 0.35004 × log10(waist + hip − neck) + 0.22100 × log10(height)) − 450</code>. All lengths use the same unit (inches in the original equation; the tool handles metric conversion for you).</p>

    <h2>How to Measure Correctly</h2>
    <p>Accuracy depends entirely on good measurements. Use a flexible tape, keep it level and parallel to the floor, and pull it snug without denting the skin. Measure the neck just below the Adam's apple, the waist across the navel (men) or at the narrowest point (women), and the hips at the widest point of the buttocks. Take each measurement twice and average them — a half-inch error at the waist can shift the estimate by more than a full percent.</p>

    <h2>Interpreting Your Result</h2>
    <p>Body fat percentage tells you what portion of your total weight is fat versus lean mass (muscle, bone, water, organs). Two people can weigh the same yet look very different because one carries more muscle. That is why body fat is often a more useful goal than the number on the scale: dropping from 24% to 18% while holding weight steady means you traded fat for muscle. Track the trend across weeks rather than fixating on a single reading.</p>

    <h2>Limitations and When to Seek Professional Testing</h2>
    <p>The Navy method assumes an average body-fat distribution, so it can misjudge bodybuilders, pregnant women, and people with unusual proportions. For a precise clinical figure, methods like DEXA scanning, hydrostatic weighing, or the Bod Pod are the gold standard. This calculator is intended for general education and self-tracking only — for medical decisions, weight-management programs, or if you have a health condition, consult a doctor or registered dietitian.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
